  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<p>A rocket, carrying a space telescope, is launched vertically upwards from the surface of the Earth. Assume that acceleration is constant. The mass of the rocket is 5.0 <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\times\"></span> 10<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"^{4}\"></span> kg and the mass of the space telescope is 9.7 <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\times\"></span> 10<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"^{3}\"></span> kg.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>a. During the first stage of the launch the rocket reaches a height of 29 km in 52 seconds. </p>\n<p>i. Show that the acceleration of the rocket during the first stage of the launch is 21 m s<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"^{-2}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>  [2]</p>\n<p>ii. Calculate the momentum of the rocket at the end of the first stage.</p>\n<p>  [2]</p>\n<p>b.  Draw a fully-labelled free-body diagram to represent the forces acting on the rocket at the moment when it reaches a height of 2 km.</p>\n<p>  [2]</p>\n<p> c.</p>\n<p>i. Show that the average net force acting on the rocket during the first stage of its motion is approximately 1.3 MN.</p>\n<p>  [2]</p>\n<p>ii. The force produced by the engine of the rocket is 2.1 MN. Calculate the average force produced by the air resistance on the rocket.</p>\n<p>  [3]</p>\n<p></p>\n\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p>"
  },
